# TheCoach custom dev workflow — replaces the stock Antfarm `feature-dev`
# bundle, which proved too fragile in trial (see
# local/cursor_loop/OPENCLAW_ANTFARM_LEARNINGS.md for the full postmortem).
#
# Design goals, each traceable to a specific finding or project rule:
#   - Never let a step "discover" the target repo (root cause of the
#     wrong-target-repo bug) — REPO is parsed from the task text and
#     asserted, not explored-and-guessed.
#   - Match TheCoach's own six-field-plus task format (_SSoT/tasks/TEMPLATE_TASK.md)
#     and protected-path rules (_SSoT/cursor_loop/DESIGN.md).
#   - Add a real live-browser QA step (Claude-in-Chrome) — neither Antfarm's
#     stock verify/test steps nor Bernstein's Janitor do this, and this
#     project's QA process (_SSoT/topics/TESTING_PROCESS.md) requires it.
#   - Every reply-format label is unique across the whole workflow (no two
#     steps share a KEY: name) to avoid the stock bundle's
#     wrong-step-label-copied bug.
#
# Specialist-tier escalation — REMOVED after the 2026-08-13 smoke test.
# The developer specified: retry-cap exhaustion -> one Specialist-tier
# attempt with full failure history -> then human triage. This is NOT
# achievable inside a single Antfarm run: advancePipeline() is strictly
# linear by step_index (confirmed from src/installer/step-ops.ts) — there
# is no conditional step branching. A `specialist-fix` step listed in the
# array runs unconditionally every time, not just on failure (confirmed
# empirically — see OPENCLAW_ANTFARM_LEARNINGS.md's session-2 entry). And
# on_fail.on_exhausted.escalate_to is a NOTIFICATION-ONLY side channel
# (sends a chat message to a human/main session or a named agent via
# sendSessionMessage) — it cannot redirect execution to another step, and
# only "main"/"human"/an "agent:..." reference resolve to anything; a bare
# step id silently no-ops. What this workflow does instead: `implement`'s
# own on_fail.on_exhausted.escalate_to is set to "main", which sends a
# real chat notification when a story's retries are exhausted. Getting an
# automated Specialist-tier retry would require either a genuinely
# different model/prompt on the SAME developer step's own retry attempts
# (not investigated this session), or a human/Architect manually
# re-pointing a stronger model at the specific failed story and running
# `antfarm workflow resume <run-id>` — semi-manual, not push-button.

id: thecoach-dev
name: TheCoach Custom Dev Workflow
version: 1
description: |
  Story-based execution pipeline for TheCoach. Planner parses the task's
  explicit REPO field and decomposes work into ordered stories. Setup
  prepares the environment against the asserted repo. Developer implements
  each story (with tests) in a fresh session, committing per story.
  Verifier checks each story AND enforces the protected-path gate. Tester
  runs integration/E2E checks. Browser QA does live Claude-in-Chrome
  verification against the task's own QA checklist. PR opens the pull
  request. Reviewer does independent code review, a live spot-check, and
  approves or requests changes via gh. A coding step that exhausts its
  retries sends a real chat notification (no automated Specialist-tier
  retry — see the note at the top of this file) and the run is left for
  human triage.

polling:
  # Never leave this as "default" — confirmed in trial it can silently
  # resolve to an unconfigured OpenAI provider and fail every run.
  model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
  timeoutSeconds: 2100  # raised from 120s — must outlive the sessions_yield wait for a spawned child (default 30 min/1800s) per the reliability patch, see ANTFARM_RELIABILITY_ROADMAP.md Phase 4

agents:
  - id: planner
    name: Planner
    role: analysis
    model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
    description: >
      Parses REPO/BRANCH/task fields from the task text and decomposes
      into ordered stories. Never explores-and-guesses the repo.
    workspace:
      baseDir: agents/planner
      files:
        AGENTS.md: agents/planner/AGENTS.md
        SOUL.md: agents/planner/SOUL.md
        IDENTITY.md: agents/planner/IDENTITY.md

  - id: setup
    name: Setup
    role: coding
    model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
    description: Asserts the repo, creates the branch, establishes baseline.
    workspace:
      baseDir: agents/setup
      files:
        AGENTS.md: agents/setup/AGENTS.md
        SOUL.md: agents/setup/SOUL.md
        IDENTITY.md: agents/setup/IDENTITY.md

  - id: developer
    name: Developer
    role: coding
    model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
    description: Implements one story at a time, with tests, committing per story.
    workspace:
      baseDir: agents/developer
      files:
        AGENTS.md: agents/developer/AGENTS.md
        SOUL.md: agents/developer/SOUL.md
        IDENTITY.md: agents/developer/IDENTITY.md

  - id: verifier
    name: Verifier
    role: verification
    model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
    description: >
      Per-story sanity check AND the hard protected-path gate — blocks
      regardless of what else passed if the diff touches _SSoT/**,
      supabase/migrations/**, or anything outside the task's declared
      Expected files.
    workspace:
      baseDir: agents/verifier
      files:
        AGENTS.md: agents/verifier/AGENTS.md
        SOUL.md: agents/verifier/SOUL.md
        IDENTITY.md: agents/verifier/IDENTITY.md

  - id: tester
    name: Tester
    role: testing
    model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
    description: Integration and E2E testing after all stories are implemented.
    workspace:
      baseDir: agents/tester
      files:
        AGENTS.md: agents/tester/AGENTS.md
        SOUL.md: agents/tester/SOUL.md
        IDENTITY.md: agents/tester/IDENTITY.md

  - id: browser-qa
    name: Browser QA
    role: testing
    model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
    description: >
      Live-app verification via Claude-in-Chrome against the task's own
      QA checklist and RTL/Hebrew coverage rows — not a lint/type/test
      proxy.
    workspace:
      baseDir: agents/browser-qa
      skills:
        - agent-browser
      files:
        AGENTS.md: agents/browser-qa/AGENTS.md
        SOUL.md: agents/browser-qa/SOUL.md
        IDENTITY.md: agents/browser-qa/IDENTITY.md

  - id: pr
    name: PR
    role: pr
    model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
    description: Opens the pull request once tester and browser-qa both pass.
    workspace:
      baseDir: agents/pr
      files:
        AGENTS.md: agents/pr/AGENTS.md
        SOUL.md: agents/pr/SOUL.md
        IDENTITY.md: agents/pr/IDENTITY.md

  - id: reviewer
    name: Reviewer
    # NOT "analysis" — that role's policy explicitly denies group:ui
    # (no browser/canvas), which would silently strip this agent's
    # ability to do the live spot-check its own job requires. "testing"
    # grants browser + web_search/web_fetch and still denies write/edit,
    # which matches what a reviewer actually needs (read + browser +
    # exec, never write). Confirmed from Antfarm's own ROLE_POLICIES in
    # src/installer/install.ts, not assumed.
    role: testing
    model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
    description: >
      Independent code review + a live spot-check, approves or requests
      changes via gh. On requested changes, the fix goes back through PR
      again (re-push, re-review) — not straight to browser-qa.
    workspace:
      baseDir: agents/reviewer
      skills:
        - agent-browser
      files:
        AGENTS.md: agents/reviewer/AGENTS.md
        SOUL.md: agents/reviewer/SOUL.md
        IDENTITY.md: agents/reviewer/IDENTITY.md

steps:
  - id: plan
    agent: planner
    input: |
      Decompose the following task into ordered user stories for autonomous
      execution. This task text MUST begin with an explicit `REPO:` line
      and a `BRANCH:` line — if either is missing or the REPO path does not
      exist / is not a git repository when you check it, STOP immediately
      and reply with STATUS: blocked and a REASON explaining exactly what's
      wrong. Do not guess a repo. Do not explore for one. Do not proceed
      without both fields present and REPO verified.

      TASK:
      {{task}}

      Instructions:
      1. Parse REPO and BRANCH from the task text above (they are the
         first two lines).
      2. Run `git -C <REPO> rev-parse --show-toplevel` and confirm the
         result matches REPO exactly (resolve any symlinks/relative paths
         first). If it doesn't match, or the command errors, STOP — reply
         STATUS: blocked, not STATUS: done.
      3. Explore the codebase AT THAT VERIFIED PATH ONLY to understand
         stack, conventions, and patterns relevant to the task.
      4. Break the task into small user stories (max 20).
      5. Order by dependency: schema/DB first, backend, frontend, integration.
      6. Each story must fit in one developer session (one context window).
      7. Every acceptance criterion must be mechanically verifiable.
      8. Always include "Typecheck passes" as the last criterion in every story.
      9. Every story MUST include test criteria — "Tests for [feature] pass".
      10. The developer is expected to write tests as part of each story.
      11. Cross-check the task's own "Protected — do not modify" list (if
          present in the task text) and make sure no story requires
          touching a protected path. If the task's requirements seem to
          require it, STOP and reply STATUS: blocked with a REASON — this
          is an Open Question for the developer, not something to route
          around.

      Reply with EXACTLY these labels (STATUS is always required; the
      others only on STATUS: done):
      STATUS: done | blocked
      REASON: (only if blocked — what's wrong, verbatim)
      REPO: /absolute/path/to/repo (only if done — the VERIFIED path)
      BRANCH: feature-branch-name (only if done)
      STORIES_JSON: [ ... array of {id,title,description,acceptanceCriteria} ... ] (only if done)
    expects: "STATUS:"
    max_retries: 1

  - id: setup
    agent: setup
    input: |
      REPO: {{repo}}
      BRANCH: {{branch}}

      Instructions:
      1. `cd {{repo}}` — do not proceed if this directory doesn't exist or
         isn't a git repo (run `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` and compare
         to {{repo}} before doing anything else; STOP and reply
         STATUS: blocked if it doesn't match, do not improvise a
         different path).
      2. `git fetch origin && git checkout main && git pull`
      3. `git checkout -b {{branch}}`
      4. Discover build/test commands (package.json scripts, CI config,
         test config files).
      5. Run the build command. Run the test command. Report real results.
      6. Report what you found.

      Reply with:
      STATUS: done | blocked
      REASON: (only if blocked)
      BUILD_CMD: (only if done)
      TEST_CMD: (only if done)
      BASELINE: build passes / tests pass, or describe what failed (only if done)
    expects: "STATUS:"
    max_retries: 2

  - id: implement
    agent: developer
    type: loop
    loop:
      over: stories
      completion: all_done
      freshSession: true
      verifyEach: true
      verifyStep: verify
    input: |
      REPO: {{repo}}
      BRANCH: {{branch}}
      BUILD_CMD: {{build_cmd}}
      TEST_CMD: {{test_cmd}}

      STORY:
      {{current_story}}

      Read _SSoT/DEV.md's Standards section (RTL/Hebrew, TypeScript strict,
      RLS on every trainer/trainee-scoped table, no magic values, shared
      logic in the shared package) before writing any code. Implement
      exactly this story, nothing more — no unrelated refactors, no scope
      creep. Write tests as part of this story (per _SSoT/topics/TESTING_PROCESS.md
      Layer 1 — run them, report the real result, "should work" is never
      acceptable). Commit your work with
      `git add -A && git commit -m "[thecoach-dev] <story id>: <short description>"`
      before finishing — this gives the verifier and protected-path gate an
      isolated diff for this story specifically, same reasoning as
      _SSoT/cursor_loop/DESIGN.md's per-task checkpoint commits.

      Reply with:
      STATUS: done | blocked
      REASON: (only if blocked)
      CHANGES: what you did, file by file (only if done)
      TEST_RESULT: the real output of running TEST_CMD just now (only if done)
      COMMIT_SHA: the commit you just made (only if done)
    expects: "STATUS:"
    max_retries: 2
    on_fail:
      max_retries: 2
      # NOT a pipeline branch — Antfarm has no support for conditional step
      # branching (advancePipeline() is strictly linear by step_index,
      # confirmed from src/installer/step-ops.ts). on_exhausted.escalate_to
      # is a NOTIFICATION-ONLY mechanism (sends a chat message via
      # sendSessionMessage to a human/main session or a named agent) — it
      # cannot run a different workflow step. "main" is the only value that
      # actually resolves to something real (per resolveEscalationTarget());
      # a bare step id like the "specialist-fix" this used to say silently
      # resolves to nothing and no-ops. Confirmed empirically in the
      # 2026-08-13 smoke test: specialist-fix, when listed as a normal
      # array step, ran unconditionally every time regardless of whether
      # implement actually failed — see OPENCLAW_ANTFARM_LEARNINGS.md.
      on_exhausted:
        escalate_to: main

  - id: verify
    agent: verifier
    type: loop
    loop:
      over: stories
      completion: all_done
    input: |
      REPO: {{repo}}
      BRANCH: {{branch}}

      STORY:
      {{current_story}}

      CLAIMED CHANGES:
      {{changes}}

      COMMIT: {{commit_sha}}

      Instructions — do BOTH checks below, in order. The protected-path
      gate runs regardless of whether the sanity check passes, and a gate
      failure overrides everything else.

      1. PROTECTED-PATH GATE (unconditional, run first):
         `git -C {{repo}} diff --stat main...{{commit_sha}}`
         Compare the touched-file list against:
           - `_SSoT/**` (never writable by a Dev agent)
           - `supabase/migrations/**`
           - anything outside this story's own claimed CHANGES
         If ANY touched file matches a protected path, or falls outside
         the claimed CHANGES, this is a GATE_FAIL regardless of whether
         the story's actual work looks correct — do not let a clean
         implementation excuse a protected-path violation.

      2. SANITY CHECK: did the developer actually do the work? Read the
         diff for this commit specifically
         (`git -C {{repo}} show {{commit_sha}}`), confirm it matches the
         claimed CHANGES, confirm tests were actually added (not just
         claimed), and confirm TEST_RESULT looks like a real run (not a
         paraphrase).

      Reply with:
      GATE: pass | fail
      GATE_REASON: (only if fail — exact offending path(s))
      STATUS: pass | fail
      STATUS_REASON: (only if fail — what's wrong, specific enough to act on)
    expects: "GATE:"
    max_retries: 1

  - id: test
    agent: tester
    input: |
      REPO: {{repo}}
      BRANCH: {{branch}}
      BUILD_CMD: {{build_cmd}}
      TEST_CMD: {{test_cmd}}

      All stories are implemented and individually verified. Run
      integration/E2E checks across the whole branch — not just the
      per-story unit tests already run. Run the full test suite and the
      build one more time against the final state of the branch. Report
      real results.

      Reply with:
      STATUS: pass | fail
      FAILURES: (only if fail — specifics)
      INTEGRATION_RESULT: real output of the integration/E2E run (only if pass)
      BUILD_RESULT: real output of the final build (only if pass)
    expects: "STATUS:"
    max_retries: 2

  - id: browser-qa
    agent: browser-qa
    input: |
      REPO: {{repo}}
      BRANCH: {{branch}}

      The branch has passed automated checks. Now do LIVE verification —
      this is Layer 2 of _SSoT/topics/TESTING_PROCESS.md, and per
      topics/TOOLING.md's live-verification standing rule, this is the
      real gate, not the automated checks above. Start the local dev
      server (and local Supabase stack if this task touches data) per
      _SSoT/DEV.md's local-dev workflow, then use the browser to actually
      exercise the task's own QA checklist end to end:

      TASK (includes the QA checklist to run):
      {{task}}

      Cover, at minimum: the task's own QA checklist items exactly as
      written (action -> observable result, no "verify the code"), and —
      mandatory per _SSoT/DEV.md, not optional — that every touched
      screen still renders correctly right-to-left with real Hebrew copy,
      not just that it renders. If ANY checklist item fails, this is a
      real fail, not a note for later — report it precisely enough that a
      fix can be attempted without re-deriving what broke.

      Reply with:
      STATUS: pass | fail
      QA_RESULTS: each checklist item and its real observed result (only if pass)
      FAILURES: exactly which item(s) failed and what actually happened, not the expected result (only if fail)
    expects: "STATUS:"
    max_retries: 1

  - id: pr
    agent: pr
    input: |
      REPO: {{repo}}
      BRANCH: {{branch}}

      All checks passed (unit, integration, live browser QA). Push the
      branch and open a PR via `gh`.

      TASK SUMMARY:
      {{task}}

      Reply with:
      STATUS: done | blocked
      REASON: (only if blocked)
      PR_URL: the real URL gh returned (only if done)
    expects: "STATUS:"
    max_retries: 2

  - id: review
    agent: reviewer
    input: |
      REPO: {{repo}}
      BRANCH: {{branch}}
      PR_URL: {{pr_url}}

      Independently review this PR — you did not write this code, review
      it as if you're seeing it for the first time. Read the full diff via
      `gh pr diff {{pr_url}}`. Check it against the original task's
      requirements and acceptance criteria, and against _SSoT/DEV.md's
      Standards (no magic values, RLS on trainer/trainee-scoped tables,
      RTL/Hebrew correctness, TypeScript strict). Do a live spot-check of
      at least one non-trivial claim from the PR description yourself
      rather than trusting it — same live-verification rule as browser-qa.

      TASK:
      {{task}}

      If you find a real problem, use `gh pr review {{pr_url}} --request-changes`
      with specific, actionable comments — this sends the work back through
      `pr` again (re-push, re-review), not straight back to browser-qa,
      per the developer's own instruction on this ("send it back to pr").
      If it's genuinely good, `gh pr review {{pr_url}} --approve`.

      Reply with:
      STATUS: approved | changes_requested
      NOTES: your real findings either way
    expects: "STATUS:"
    max_retries: 1
    on_fail:
      retry_step: pr
